import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import ModellingVisual from '@/components/modelling/ModellingVisual.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="Things and identities: which property do you mean?" description="An address can connect several records without making them records of the same thing. A useful model helps people recognise those differences before information is combined.">
  <section aria-labelledby="one-address">
    <h2 id="one-address">One address. More than one thing.</h2>
    <p>Imagine a report arriving with the subject “14 Orchard Road”. It sounds precise. But our fictional Harbour Court building contains Flat 1 and Flat 2. Does the report concern the whole building, one dwelling, or an entry in another record? The address helps us find the situation; it does not answer the question.</p>
    <p>Linked data gives us a way to identify these things separately and explain their relationships. We can say that the building contains the dwellings, that a report describes an inspection and that the inspection concerned a particular subject. We do not have to press all those meanings into one field called “property”.</p>
  </section>

<figure class="modelling-figure" data-modelling-figure="identity-map">
    <ModellingVisual kind="identity-map" />
    <dl>
      <dt>The building</dt><dd>Harbour Court, containing two dwellings in this fictional case.</dd>
      <dt>The dwellings</dt><dd>Flat 1 and Flat 2: two possible inspection subjects, not interchangeable labels.</dd>
      <dt>The address</dt><dd>14 Orchard Road: useful descriptive information, not proof that all associated resources are identical.</dd>
      <dt>The title record</dt><dd>A separately identified record, connected to the property situation but not equated with the building.</dd>
    </dl>
    <figcaption>All examples are fictional teaching commitments. This is not an approved property-identity model or a legal description of a real title.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="same">
    <h2 id="same">“Connected” is not “the same”</h2>
    <p>Suppose the title record and the building description contain exactly the same address. Combining their information may be useful. Declaring them to be the same thing would be a mistake: a record and the physical building have different identities. The right connection preserves the distinction rather than deleting it.</p>
    <p>This is why matching words and identifiers are evidence to examine, not automatic permission to merge. Ask what each identifier identifies. A building identifier, dwelling identifier and report reference may all be useful, but each serves a different question.</p>
    <aside class="callout callout--note modelling-callout">
      <div class="callout__label">What would the technical mistake look like?</div>
      <p>We explicitly refuse <code>owl:sameAs</code> between the example building and title record. That predicate expresses identity, not “related to”, “mentions the same address” or “probably a useful match”. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/classes-and-relationships">classes and relationships chapter</a> explains the formal distinction.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="change">
    <h2 id="change">Ask what changes—and what stays the same</h2>
    <p>The Harbour Court inspection happens on 12 August 2026. Report version 1 appears on 14 August. A correction produces report version 2 on 20 August. There is a new report version, not automatically a new inspection. A “latest date” field that hides this distinction can give a convincing but wrong answer.</p>
    <p>Other changes are harder. If Flat 1 is subdivided into two dwellings, does the original dwelling continue? We deliberately leave that question open here. Practitioners must agree the rule for recognising the dwelling through that change; a fresh label or a “converted” status does not settle it.</p>
    <p>The important habit is to name the hard case. Guarino and Welty’s <a href="https://www.loa.istc.cnr.it/old/Papers/CACM2002.pdf"><cite>Evaluating Ontological Decisions with OntoClean</cite>, 2002, pp. 62–63</a> explains why identity criteria help expose apparently natural but mistaken classifications. Our property cases are illustrations of that questioning discipline, not examples authored by them.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="roles">
    <h2 id="roles">Separate identity, role and state with three questions</h2>
    <p>
      These words name different tests, not boxes to memorise. A
      <a href="/glossary#kind">kind</a> asks what sort of thing is being identified. A
      <a href="/glossary#role">role</a> describes how something participates in a
      relationship or setting. A <a href="/glossary#phase">phase</a> is a classification
      that can change while the same subject continues. The exact analysis belongs in
      the formal method; first ask what the example requires.
    </p>
    <figure class="modelling-figure" aria-labelledby="role-caption" data-modelling-figure="role-phase">
      <figcaption id="role-caption">One person, two transaction roles—and a separate record-state question</figcaption>
      <ModellingVisual kind="role-phase" />
      <ol class="modelling-steps">
        <li>
          <h3>What is being identified?</h3>
          <p>The same illustrative person remains identifiable independently of one sale. Ask what establishes that identity; an unchanged name is not enough.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>In what setting does the description apply?</h3>
          <p>The person acts as seller in transaction A and buyer in transaction B. Each statement needs its transaction and relevant period. These are contextual roles, not different people.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>What changes while the subject continues?</h3>
          <p>For this deliberately stipulated example, one review record continues while its workflow label changes from draft to reviewed. Ask which subject the state concerns and what supports continuing identity.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Time alone does not supply the answer. Subdivision of Flat 1 is not a phase merely
      because it happens over time. A report correction is not automatically a phase of
      the inspection. Nor do the shortcuts “kind means permanent”, “role means temporary”
      or “every status is a phase” replace the identity and dependence questions.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout callout--note modelling-callout">
      <div class="callout__label">Fictional finance transfer</div>
      <p>Suppose one person is a borrower in arrangement A and a guarantor in arrangement B. What relationship, period and evidence make each role meaningful? More than one answer may be defensible, or the evidence may be insufficient. This asks a modelling question; it does not state an agreed lending rule.</p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      A seller role identifies participation in a transaction; it does not prove
      permission to read every linked report. That handling question needs its own
      authority and evidence. For the more exact tests and formal representation, use
      <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/roles-and-phases">roles and phases in Ontology modelling</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next">
    <h2 id="next">Use the distinction in a review</h2>
    <p>When reading a definition, try three questions: what exactly does this identify; what related thing must not be confused with it; and which change would challenge its identity? A precise counterexample can be more useful than a long list of additional fields.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="source-contract">
    <h2 id="source-contract">What this explanation rests on</h2>
    <p>OPDA <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0047">ODR-0047</a>, <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0041">ODR-0041</a> / <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0042">ODR-0042</a> and <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0059">ODR-0059</a> inform the identity and relationship discipline; <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0053">ODR-0053</a> distinguishes temporal state and recording. Adoption provenance: upstream revision <code>67174057e6384b79d0b28b7736fe70a66e112895</code>, 5 September 2026. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/standards-and-decisions">decision and provenance register</a> separates method authority from OPDA candidate evidence. Harbour Court is an illustration, not a conformance claim.</p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
